What are Assertions?
Assertions verify that something is true and 'expect' is used for assertions

Example:

Login button clicked
      ↓
Dashboard appears
      ↓
Verify dashboard is visible

1. toBeVisible() ⭐ Most Common
await expect(
  page.getByText('Welcome')
).toBeVisible();

Meaning:
Verify that "Welcome" is visible on the page.



2. toHaveText()

HTML:
<h1>Dashboard</h1>
TypeScript:

await expect(
  page.locator('h1')
).toHaveText('Dashboard');

Meaning:
Verify exact text.


3. toContainText()
await expect(
  page.locator('h1')
).toContainText('Dash'); // dashboard ka dash

Meaning:
Verify partial text.



4. toBeHidden()
await expect(
  page.getByText('Loading...')
).toBeHidden();

Meaning:
Verify element is not visible.

5. toBeChecked()

Checkbox example:
await expect(
  page.getByRole('checkbox')
).toBeChecked();

Meaning:
Verify checkbox is checked.


6. toHaveValue()

Input field:
await page
  .getByRole('textbox')
  .fill('Kirti');

Verify:
await expect(
  page.getByRole('textbox')
).toHaveValue('Kirti');


7. toHaveURL()

After login:
await expect(page)
  .toHaveURL('https://app.com/dashboard');

Meaning:
Verify current URL.

8. toHaveTitle()
await expect(page)
  .toHaveTitle('Dashboard');

Meaning:
Verify page title.




Real Login Example
await page.goto('https://example.com');

await page
  .getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Username' })
  .fill('admin');

await page
  .getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' })
  .click();

await expect(
  page.getByText('Dashboard')
).toBeVisible();

This is a real test.
Without the last line, Playwright just clicks.
With the last line, Playwright verifies success.


